EU economics and finance ministers are  set to approve recommendations on  national reform programs for member  countries in 2014 and on the economic  policies of the euro area the moves part  of the European semester process a cycle  that helps EU Member States balance  their budgets and improve their fiscal  and economic policies the council is  likely to approve draft recommendations  to help bolster the economic policies of  26 of the 28 EU countries and a specific  recommendation on the euro area these  recommendations will be forwarded to the  General Affairs Council with a view to  being endorsed by EU heads of state and  government on jun 26th and 27th the  ministers are also due to approve a  proposal to allow Lithuania to adopt the  euro as its currency from the first of  January next year which would make it  the 19th member of the group in other  items on the agenda the council will try  to agree a measure to counter the double  non taxation of corporate groups  deriving from hybrid loan arrangements  the aims to close a tax loophole that  allows companies to exploit mismatches  between national tax rules to avoid  paying taxes on some types of profits  distributed within the group
 

